# Per-finding suppressions for rite's publish gate, one entry per line:
#
#     <fingerprint>  # <reason>
#
# `rite publish check` prints the fingerprint to copy. A reason is required; an
# entry without one is a parse error, not a silent no-op.
#
# WHY THERE ARE ANY. Every entry below is one of the gate's always-on
# hardcoded-home-directory rules, and every covered line is already on `main`.
# The gate demotes a pre-existing finding to a warning only when the push does
# not touch its file, so a change to any of these eight files surfaces every
# home-shaped path that file already contained. There are around 400 more in the
# rest of the tree, and the same block comes back for whoever touches these files
# next: DF241 in docs/contributors/design/findings-unresolved.md.
#
# WHAT AN ENTRY COVERS. The fingerprint's last field is `sha256-<digest>` of
# what was found, so an entry is scoped to (file, rule, matched string) and NOT
# to a line — deliberately, because the line form goes stale when anything above
# it moves. One entry therefore covers every occurrence of that string in that
# file, including ones added later; the gate prints `one entry covers N findings`
# but never escalates on N growing. Read that line.
#
# Each reason below was written from the reported line, not from the file name.
# None of the matched text is a secret. Two of them are a contributor's own name
# used as an example, and say so.

